specific ranges for sort and tail, so I should structure the tasks to have the agent read the SRD directly and implement those designated requirement IDs. Since each task needs to be independently executable, I'll make sure to reference the spec files and source locations clearly, then list out the four task breakdowns using the git log title pattern I established. Looking at the weight constraints more carefully—the system defaults to weight 1 per requirement and caps total task weight at 4, so my three-requirement approach gives me flexibility. I'm uncertain whether release 07.0 follows the same weight-4 isolation pattern as later releases, so I'll stick with this conservative estimate to avoid violations.\n\nNow let me recalculate the task count based on the actual ready requirements across these SRDs. I'm seeing 3 ready requirements for srd054-tr's R3 group, 3 for R4, 3 for srd053-sort, and multiple groups for srd055-tail including one with 4 ready requirements.\n\nGiven the 4-task limit, I can fit srd054-tr split into two dependent tasks, plus srd053-sort and srd055-tail's first group independently. That leaves the remaining tail requirements for a future batch, which works fine within the constraints.\n\nLet me verify the ordering rules apply correctly to this task arrangement. I'm verifying the alphabetical ordering holds up with the dependency constraints—since all tasks are in the same release level, I'll stick with the file path ordering and then handle the tr dependencies by placing R4 after R3. Now I need to construct the actual task descriptions with the right file references and dependencies for each one. I'm mapping out the remaining tasks—Task 2 covers the tr command with requirements R3.1 through R3.3 from srd054, and Task 3 handles R4.1 through R4.3 from the same spec. Since I don't have access to the actual SRD file contents, I'll structure the task descriptions to point agents to the requirements by ID while keeping the format consistent with what I've already outlined. I see the git log shows the pattern includes a descriptive subtitle like \"character classes and SET extensions\" for the tr command, but I don't have those names for the other requirement groups. I'll stick with just the SRD reference format for consistency. Now I need to set up the YAML with the required reading materials — the SRD file for each utility, the existing implementation, tests, and the project documentation.\n\nFor the files to modify, I'm targeting the main.go and the test file for each utility. But there's a tension here: the guidelines say to always split implementation and tests into separate tasks, decomposing into types, implementation, and tests. However, if I do that, each task becomes too small and falls below the minimum requirements. Let me check what the existing task pattern actually shows.\n\nLooking at the git history, Task 5382 combines both implementation and tests in a single task, modifying both main.go and the test file together. The commit covers four requirements and treats the differential tests as part of the same work since they're just adding test case entries to an existing test structure. Given that the tests here are straightforward additions rather than complex independent artifacts, I think following the project's established pattern of combining them makes more sense than artificially splitting them.\n\nBut the planning constitution is explicit: implementation and tests must always be separate tasks, with a maximum of three task types per feature. This directly conflicts with what the project has been doing. The constraint is clear that a single task cannot both implement and test the same feature.\n\nThe real problem is that splitting implementation from tests would require six tasks for three requirements, but I can only propose four tasks total. So I need to figure out how differential tests fit into this constraint—whether they count as \"testing the same feature\" or if they're treated diffe